        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(android): platform trust for public gateway certs + manual fingerprint pinning",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe Android app pins a TLS fingerprint for every gateway, including hosts with publicly valid certificates. For a Tailscale Funnel gateway on `*.ts.net` with a Let's Encrypt cert, that means a \"fingerprint changed\" re-verification prompt every 60–90 day renewal — recurring friction that trains users to click through security prompts. Separately, when the TLS probe failed with no stored pin, connecting hard-failed with no escape hatch, and a malformed stored pin silently degraded instead of failing closed.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- **Platform trust for public certs.** Before prompting, the probe performs a real handshake against the system CA store with `endpointIdentificationAlgorithm=\"HTTPS\"` and SNI. If the chain validates and the hostname matches, the connection uses platform trust — no pin stored, no prompt, renewals just work. A conservative candidate classifier (rejects `.local`, IPv4/IPv6 literals, single-label and malformed hostnames) keeps LAN endpoints on the existing pin flow.\n- **Existing pins stay authoritative.** A valid public chain never silently clears a user-accepted pin. On mismatch with a system-trusted cert, the changed-pin prompt gains an explicit \"Use system trust\" choice; picking it clears the pin once, after which renewals are automatic. Replacing a pin always requires explicit confirmation.\n- **Discovery cannot become identity.** Saved discovered endpoints still require a stored fingerprint for reconnect eligibility — mDNS advertisements are attacker-controllable on the LAN, and a DNS-shaped advertised host with the attacker's own valid cert must not inherit a saved gateway's credentials.\n- **Out-of-band manual pinning.** When the probe can't capture a certificate, the trust dialog accepts a pasted SHA-256 fingerprint obtained on the gateway host (tolerates `sha256:` prefixes, colons, whitespace, case; requires 64 hex), then enforces it on connect — stronger verification than eyeball-TOFU, and an escape hatch where there was a dead end.\n- Hardening along the way: malformed stored pins now fail closed at handshake time; both probe attempts share one connect+handshake budget so a TCP-accepting, TLS-stalling host cannot double probe latency; the trust decision is a closed sealed type (`SystemTrusted` / `PinnedTrust` / `PromptRequired` / `Failed`).\n\n## User Impact\n\nFunnel/public-cert gateways connect without fingerprint ceremony and stop re-prompting on cert renewals. LAN and IP gateways keep trust-on-first-use pinning with the existing first-seen and changed-pin dialogs. Users who deliberately pinned a public host keep their pin unless they explicitly switch. Two new user-facing strings, translated across all 21 locales.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `GatewayTlsTest` (trust-decision matrix incl. supersession rows, candidate classifier, fingerprint normalizer, probe-budget splitting), `GatewayBootstrapAuthTest`, `GatewaySessionCustomHeadersTest`: green on both Play and ThirdParty flavors (14 + 51 + 2 per flavor).\n- `:app:ktlintCheck`, `android-app-i18n` check, `git diff --check`: green.\n- Structured autoreview: three rounds; four accepted findings fixed (two P1s — discovery-eligibility identity binding, silent pin supersession — plus fail-closed pin handling and probe-latency amplification); final round clean: \"consistently adds Android platform trust for eligible DNS gateways while preserving existing certificate pins.\"\n- All `resolveTlsParams` call sites verified `allowTOFU=false`, so the system-trust path connects with platform trust and real hostname verification (no TOFU fallback).\n",
        "branch": "feat/android-tls-system-trust",
